WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:07.230
Let's translate various film terminology and best practices into those applicable to game development.

2
00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:13.230
On the left is a list of various actors, components, and things Unreal Engine includes.

3
00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:19.230
It's important to note that many of the items can be added to and manipulated within a sequence.

4
00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:23.230
Control Rig, for example, is predominantly animated through Sequencer.

5
00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:28.230
On the right are various terminologies we use when working with sequences.

6
00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:36.229
Scene Assemblies are the complete encapsulated collection of actors, animations, and logic nested within a sequence.

7
00:00:36.229 --> 00:00:43.229
Performance Takes is a feature that allows you to record animation from motion capture performances or input devices

8
00:00:43.229 --> 00:00:48.229
using an iterative workflow to automatically record the motion to a sequence.

9
00:00:48.229 --> 00:00:56.229
This tool is an extremely useful time saver in capturing multiple variations of a single shot and can be reviewed on the fly.

10
00:00:56.229 --> 00:01:02.329
Takes can be swapped directly within a sequence via the shot properties from the drop-down menu.

11
00:01:02.329 --> 00:01:08.739
Sets, Layouts, Staging, these all refer to the assembly of our actors in our scene,

12
00:01:08.739 --> 00:01:14.739
or within our sequence via spawnables or swappables that essentially create the final look of our scene.

13
00:01:14.739 --> 00:01:22.739
Level Sequenced with Shots refers to our graduated or parent sequences within nested sequences, or shots.

14
00:01:22.739 --> 00:01:27.780
It's important to note that Shots are not a specific actor type.

15
00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:33.780
In fact, Subtracks, Shots, Nested Sequences, and Parent Tracks are all the same actor type.

16
00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:39.780
They're all sequences. It's how you use those sequences that really determines its functionality.

17
00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:44.780
On the left are various stages or assemblies when working with traditional film practices.

18
00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:50.780
On the right, we've translated those terminologies into terminology commonly used with Unreal Engine,

19
00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:57.780
specifically when working with sequences. I won't cover each line, but I do encourage you to pause the video

20
00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:06.780
and read through the slide to get more in-depth information and understanding how we translate film terminology to that that we use in Unreal.
